From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 17:16:12 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Add Db8Predictor: a DictionaryPredictor backed by webOS db8

Same idea as DictionaryPredictor - prefix-match tokens out of a word
list - except the word list lives in webOS's db8 rather than a flat
file: the user dictionary that Text Assist in
org.webosports.app.settings keeps in the kind
org.webosports.app.settings.dictionary:1. predict() only ever reads an
in-memory copy of that list, so a keystroke never waits on the bus.
Configured the same way as DictionaryPredictor, with KIND (a db8 kind
name) in place of DICTIONARY and the same PROBABILITY, and activated in
presage.xml.template as UserDictionaryPredictor.

Keeping the copy current: a find with "watch":true delivers a single
"fired" notification in practice, and re-registering the watch from its
own handler loops (over ten thousand iterations in under a second on a
device, with mojodb pinned). So the copy is refreshed by a plain 30 s
poll timer; the find+watch only stays as a fast path for the first
change after startup.

The poll timer and the watch both carry the predictor as their context,
and presage builds a new predictor for every keyboard language. Both are
removed in the destructor, before LSUnregister() drops any refresh
still in flight; a timer left behind fired into the freed predictor
30 s after a language switch and killed MaliitServer (BraX3,
13 Nov 2026). A new kind replaces the old watch instead of adding one.

luna-service2 and json-c are only available, and only needed, for the
target build. The native variant used for build-time tools such as
text2ngram must keep building without them, so detection is non-fatal
and Db8Predictor drops out of the build (USE_DB8PREDICTOR) if either is
missing, the same way USE_SQLITE does for SQLite.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS-ports specific]
---
